# ── Agent memory & handoff: the closest neighbours to our own dogfooding ─────
# Metadata below fetched 2026-08-26 from the GitHub API (script tier, zero tokens).
# LICENSE IS THE GATE: all four are permissive, so the concepts AND the expression
# are available to us with attribution. That is the exception, not the rule.

kb:comp-ai-memory rdf:type     ktype:Competitor ;
               rdfs:label   "ai-memory (akitaonrails)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:competitive-landscape ;
               hnav:order   14 ;
               kpred:repo-url     "https://github.com/akitaonrails/ai-memory" ;
               kpred:license      "MIT" ;
               kpred:copy-permitted "yes — MIT, attribution required" ;
               kpred:stars        4852 ;
               kpred:language     "Rust" ;
               kpred:last-push    "2026-08-26"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:description  "Long-term memory for coding agents, and the handoff BETWEEN them: quit Claude Code mid-task, start Codex in the same directory hours later, continue without re-explaining the architecture, the failed approaches, or the open questions. Lifecycle hooks capture bounded, sanitized observations; at session end those are compiled into a coherent summary and the next agent receives a bounded 'where you left off' block before its first prompt. The store is plain markdown in a git repo — greppable, Obsidian-openable, rsync-backed, and explicitly 'no vector database to babysit, no write_note ceremony'." ;
               kpred:closest-to   kb:workspace-index ;
               kpred:closest-to   kb:agent-context-dogfooding ;
               kpred:honest-note  "This is the closest thing in the neighbourhood to what HANDOFF.md is trying to be, and it is better at it. Their handoff is COMPILED and BOUNDED; ours is 107,738 bytes of hand-maintained prose plus a 94,002-byte archive, and the first act of the session that added this entry was to grep its section headers rather than read it. That is the failure mode, stated out loud rather than filed as a nice-to-have." ;
               kpred:worth-adopting kb:adopt-bounded-handoff ;
               kpred:worth-adopting kb:adopt-capture-allowlist ;
               kpred:validates    "Their support matrix states per-harness capability in the same sentence as the claim — 'capture works; no hook handoff injection, so recover handoffs via MCP' — instead of a blanket 'supported'. That is kb:honest-status arrived at independently, by a project with 4,852 stars and every commercial incentive to claim uniform support. Two projects reaching the same disclosure discipline is evidence the discipline is load-bearing." ;
               kpred:we-avoid     "The harness-integration surface: 25+ agent CLIs with per-vendor hooks, managed-workstream wrappers that relaunch other people's tools, and a Docker/systemd server with bearer auth and per-operator memory slots." ;
               kpred:avoid-reason "That is a different product. See kb:avoid-agent-harness-layer." .

kb:comp-megamemory rdf:type    ktype:Competitor ;
               rdfs:label   "MegaMemory (0xK3vin)" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:competitive-landscape ;
               hnav:order   15 ;
               kpred:repo-url     "https://github.com/0xK3vin/MegaMemory" ;
               kpred:license      "MIT" ;
               kpred:copy-permitted "yes — MIT, attribution required" ;
               kpred:stars        506 ;
               kpred:language     "TypeScript" ;
               kpred:last-push    "2026-05-03"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:description  "The single closest architectural match to our own stack: an MCP server over a persistent per-project knowledge graph of CONCEPTS (feature, module, pattern, config, decision, component) with typed edges (connects_to, depends_on, implements, calls, configured_by), in-process quantized embeddings (all-MiniLM-L6-v2, 384d, ONNX, no network after first download), brute-force cosine search, soft-delete that preserves history, and a d3-force web explorer. Storage is per-project SQLite. Nine MCP tools, fronted by one natural-language entry point, `understand`." ;
               kpred:closest-to   kb:mcp-server ;
               kpred:closest-to   kb:agent-context-dogfooding ;
               kpred:honest-note  "We built the same shape — MCP server, concept graph, local quantized embeddings, visual explorer — and theirs has the better-signposted tool surface: 9 tools with one obvious front door, against our 21 presented flat. Last pushed 2026-05-03, so roughly four months quiet; that is a caution about depending on it, not a reason to discount what it got right." ;
               kpred:worth-adopting kb:adopt-tool-front-door ;
               kpred:worth-adopting kb:adopt-divergence-merge ;
               kpred:validates    "Their merge engine keeps BOTH sides of a conflict as ::left/::right variants under one merge-group id and refuses to pick blindly — an agent resolving one must verify both against the current source and record the verification reason. That is kb:dichotomy's distinction (fix a conflict, PRESERVE a genuine dichotomy) reached from a different substrate, by a project that had to solve it under duress." ;
               kpred:we-avoid     "'The LLM is the indexer. No AST parsing. No static analysis.' — structural facts written by a model in its own words, with no deterministic pass underneath." ;
               kpred:avoid-reason "See kb:avoid-model-as-index." ;
               kpred:evidence     "Their README concedes the cost of the storage choice in passing: a SQLite knowledge.db 'cannot be auto-merged by git', which is why they had to build a merge CLI, conflict groups, and a resolution protocol to get back what a text format gives for free. Our TTL merges textually. That is a real, unglamorous win for the file format, worth recording precisely because we chose it for other reasons and got this one by accident." .

# ── Not competitors: a technique and a component source ──────────────────────
# Recorded here because kpred:repo-url is what competitor-scan.ts tracks, so their
# licenses stay watched. Neither is adjacent to the thesis, and saying so is the point.

kb:ref-hyperframes rdf:type    ktype:Reference ;
               rdfs:label   "HyperFrames (HeyGen) — HTML to deterministic video, built for agents" ;
               kpred:part-of      kb:competitive-landscape ;
               hnav:order   16 ;
               kpred:repo-url     "https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes" ;
               kpred:license      "Apache-2.0" ;
               kpred:copy-permitted "yes — Apache-2.0, attribution required (+ NOTICE, patent grant)" ;
               kpred:stars        42748 ;
               kpred:language     "TypeScript" ;
               kpred:last-push    "2026-08-27"^^xsd:date ;
               kpred:description  "Turns HTML, CSS, media and SEEKABLE animations into deterministic MP4 through headless Chrome: the renderer advances animation to an explicit frame time rather than recording against a wall clock, so the same input yields the same frames. Ships 20 agent skills behind a single `/hyperframes` router that installs each creation workflow on demand instead of loading all twenty." ;
               kpred:relates-to   kb:context-compression ;
               kpred:honest-note  "NOT a competitor and barely adjacent — it renders video, we render graphs. It is in this graph for two transferable techniques and for nothing else, and its 42,748 stars say nothing about us. Recording weak relevance as weak is the point: a research graph that inflates every input into a threat or an opportunity is marketing with footnotes." ;
               kpred:worth-adopting kb:adopt-skill-router ;
               kpred:evidence     "Seek-based determinism names a problem we actually have. OverlayGraph.svelte:128-129 seeds initial node positions with unseeded Math.random(), so the 2D layout is not reproducible run to run, which is why visual assertions on it must wait for a settled state instead of comparing frames. Driving time as a parameter rather than as elapsed wall clock is the shape of a fix. Nothing is copied; the idea is free and the expression is not needed." ;
               kpred:we-avoid     "Video generation as a product surface." ;
               kpred:avoid-reason "Reckons.AI has no video feature and must not acquire one in order to justify a library. If this ever earns a place it is in announcing a release, not in the app." .

